comments.message:
There is dbus-sensors code today that requires the old way (if i'm reading the code right):
```
    for (size_t ii = 0; ii < thresholdCount; ii++)
    {
        std::string thresholdInterface =
            baseInterface + ".Thresholds" + std::to_string(ii);
```

So I guess you'd need to find all of the places that do things like this and fix them to make them work both ways before it could ever be enabled?

I briefly looked into going via the schema files to construct `Type` property of nested objects by looking at schema names and property names in case of inline schemas.

But that's not easily possible in all cases, for example:
```
"MuxOutputs": {
                    "description": "The pin names and values for the mux which toggles the flash to BMC",
                    "type": "array",
                    "items": {
                        "type": "object",
                        "additionalProperties": false,
                        "properties": {
                            "Name": {
                                "$ref": "legacy.json#/$defs/Types/Name"
                            },  
                            "Polarity": {
                                "$ref": "legacy.json#/$defs/Types/Polarity"
                            }   
                        },  
                        "required": ["Name", "Polarity"]
                    }   
                },  
```

Instead, settled on hardcoding this information in a tree structure.
The tree structure can be extended as needed.

Based on that structure we know which configuration to expose in the new vs. old way. The structure is in `config_type_tree.cpp`.


Thanks for your comment here. It was some work to create the structure and adjust the patch but it will be good to reduce overall config bloat.
